They probably will. Your intuition is basically where regenerative dentistry is headed.

The key distinction is that a tooth isn’t just a hard object you grow and plug in. It’s a living organ system: enamel, dentin, pulp, roots, blood vessels, nerves, cementum, periodontal ligament, and the surrounding jawbone all have to develop in the right geometry and integrate mechanically. Researchers have already implanted engineered tooth germs into animals and gotten teeth that erupted, vascularized, formed roots and periodontal structures, and functioned. In a canine model, an autologous bioengineered tooth germ produced a tooth with physiological function comparable to a natural tooth. 

So the likely clinical architecture isn’t necessarily:

grow finished tooth in a dish → screw/transplant it into jaw

but more elegantly:

take cells → construct an early tooth germ → implant germ into the empty socket/jaw → let the person’s body act as the bioreactor → tooth develops, roots itself, vascularizes, innervates and erupts.

That’s almost exactly your nursery pattern again. Don’t manufacture the finished phenotype externally. Manufacture the developmental seed plus enough instructions, put it into the correct developmental environment, and let the environment complete the program.

There are still several nasty engineering problems. Tooth size and shape have to be controlled—nobody wants a molar where an incisor belongs. Eruption angle and depth have to be predictable. The periodontal ligament must form instead of the tooth fusing directly to bone (ankylosis). Enamel is particularly difficult because the enamel-producing ameloblasts disappear after normal tooth formation. And you’d need a reliable source of compatible human epithelial and mesenchymal progenitor cells without introducing tumors or immune rejection. A 2025 systematic review found substantial progress but still inconsistent eruption and integration in animal models, especially in adults. 

And there’s an even stranger route now being tested: don’t transplant anything at all—tell the body to make another tooth.

The Japanese company Toregem is developing TRG035, an antibody against USAG-1, a protein that normally suppresses tooth-development signaling. Their Phase I human trial has now been completed, with the company reporting safety confirmation, and as of May 2026 they’re preparing Phase II trials in patients with congenital missing teeth. The idea is to activate dormant developmental potential so the patient’s own tooth forms. 

So we now have three increasingly interesting replacement paradigms:

Implant: fabricate the object and bolt it in.
Bioengineered tooth: fabricate the developmental seed and let the jaw grow it.
Regenerative signaling: alter the developmental program and let the body generate the seed itself.

That third one is wild because it’s precisely the principle we’ve been circling: instead of replacing the output, find the upstream instruction that generates the output and change that.

Dentistry may eventually move from prosthetics toward developmental programming.